 \begin{abstract}
-Even when all clients optimize the same strongly convex objective $f$, the global model parameters produced by constant-step federated learning may have a stationary mean that differs from the minimizer $x^\star$. Does increasing the number of clients eliminate this bias, or can some components persist? Answering this question clarifies the limits of client averaging. We study \Scaf{} and matched \FedAvg{} under full participation with independent bounded additive gradient noise. We analyze the long-run global model and within-round local fluctuations to derive multidimensional bias expansions in the step size $\gamma$ and inverse client count $1/N$. Both algorithms share a $\gamma/N$ coefficient, but their client-independent $\gamma^2$ coefficients differ. The \Scaf{} coefficient combines the local-update contribution found in \FedAvg{} with an additional contribution from persistent controls. These controls cancel in the server average but change local second moments, which interact with third-order curvature to shift the stationary mean. We establish explicit coefficient formulas with a remainder of $O_{H,d}(\gamma^2/N+\gamma^3)$, uniform in $N$ for fixed $H,d$ and sufficiently small $\gamma$. This bound permits identification of the client-independent coefficients along any joint limit $\gamma\to0$, $N\to\infty$, without a relative-rate condition. Controlled experiments examine coefficient predictions and local-step dependence. Real-data model averaging shows that systematic finite-tail mean displacement can become a substantial component of parameter error. A post-hoc comparison further finds close agreement between the observed algorithmic mean-shift direction and the derived curvature--noise structure, while its magnitude and local-step scaling remain approximate.
+Even when all clients optimize the same strongly convex objective, constant-step federated learning can have a stationary mean of the global model parameters that differs from the objective minimizer. Which bias contributions are reduced by client averaging, and how do local updates and persistent controls affect those that remain? We study \Scaf{} and matched \FedAvg{} under full participation, a sufficiently smooth objective, and independent bounded additive gradient noise. Analyzing global stationary balance and within-round local fluctuations yields multidimensional expansions with a shared $\gamma/N$ term and explicit client-independent $\gamma^2$ coefficients, where $\gamma$ is the step size and $N$ the client count. The \Scaf{} coefficient combines the ordinary local-update contribution found in \FedAvg{} with an additional persistent-control contribution. Although zero-sum controls cancel from the direct server increment, they change local second moments, which interact with third-order curvature to shift the stationary mean. For fixed dimension $d$ and local-step count $H$, a remainder of $O_{H,d}(\gamma^2/N+\gamma^3)$, uniform in $N$ for sufficiently small $\gamma$, identifies the second-order coefficients along arbitrary joint small-step, large-client limits after removing the shared leading term. Controlled experiments assess these predictions. Real-data model averaging shows that finite-tail mean displacement can become a substantial part of parameter error. A post-hoc comparison also finds close directional agreement with a reference-point curvature--noise vector, while its magnitude and local-step scaling remain approximate.
 \end{abstract}
 
 
