+Client averaging and control correction act on different parts of stationary
+mean bias. We made this distinction explicit for \Scaf{} and matched \FedAvg{}:
+the algorithms share a $\gamma/N$ term, while their client-independent
+$\gamma^2$ coefficients separate ordinary local-update and persistent-control
+contributions. Local second moments coupled with third-order curvature explain
+the additional control effect, and a uniform remainder identifies the
+coefficients along arbitrary joint small-step, large-client limits. An exact
+control translation extends the \Scaf{} result to affine objective shifts.
+
+Controlled experiments examine these quantitative predictions. Real-data model
+averaging illustrates that finite-tail mean displacement can be an important
+component of parameter error, and a post-hoc full-vector comparison finds
+close directional agreement with the derived curvature--noise structure despite
+magnitude and local-step discrepancies. Together, the analysis and experiments
+explain an aspect of federated optimization that leading-order bias and
+variance alone do not distinguish.
 
